          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Gas vs. Battery vs. Corded Electric Chainsaw: Which Power Source Is Right for You?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest decision you'll make when buying the <strong>best chainsaw</strong> for your needs isn't the brand or the model — it's the power source. Each of the three categories makes real tradeoffs, and the right answer depends entirely on how much you cut, how often, and how far you are from an outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Chainsaws</h3>
          <p>
            A <strong>gas chainsaw</strong> remains the standard for anyone felling large trees, processing serious firewood volume, or working off-grid where charging a battery isn't practical. Gas engines deliver sustained, unlimited-runtime power — as long as you have fuel, you keep cutting. The tradeoffs are real, though: gas saws are the loudest option by a wide margin (often 110+ dB), require mixed fuel and regular carburetor and air filter maintenance, and involve a pull-cord start that can be genuinely frustrating in cold weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Chainsaws</h3>
          <p>
            A <strong>battery chainsaw</strong> — sometimes called a <strong>cordless chainsaw</strong> — has closed the power gap with gas remarkably in the last few years. Modern 40V-80V systems deliver chain speed and torque that rivals mid-size gas saws for most homeowner tasks: limbing, pruning, and moderate firewood processing. The appeal is obvious — instant start, near-silent operation relative to gas, and zero fuel mixing or carburetor maintenance. The real limitation is runtime, which drops meaningfully in dense hardwood versus softwood, and battery/charger cost if you're starting a platform from scratch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric Chainsaws</h3>
          <p>
            A corded electric chainsaw is the cheapest way into chainsaw ownership and offers unlimited runtime like gas, without any fuel or battery to manage. The catch is the cord itself — you're tethered to an outlet, realistically within about 100 feet using a heavy-gauge extension cord, which rules this category out for anything beyond a small suburban yard close to the house.
          </p>

