        <div id="bar-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Bar Length Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bar length is the second-most-important spec after power source, and it's the one homeowners get wrong most often — usually by buying too much saw for the job. A good rule of thumb: your bar should be at least 2 inches longer than the diameter of whatever you're cutting most often, but going dramatically oversized just adds weight and cost without benefit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12-14 Inches: Light Pruning and Limbing</h3>
          <p>
            This range is purpose-built for trimming branches, shaping ornamental trees, and clearing small deadwood — the kind of detail work where a compact, lightweight saw like the DeWalt 20V MAX XR or Ryobi 40V compact shines. Anything larger and you're carrying unnecessary weight for the task at hand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">16-18 Inches: The Typical Homeowner Sweet Spot</h3>
          <p>
            For most homeowners cutting firewood, clearing storm debris, and handling trees up to about 16 inches in diameter, this is the range that covers the widest variety of jobs without overbuying. Most of the saws we recommend in this guide, including our overall favorites, fall in this bracket for good reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">20+ Inches: Felling and Large-Diameter Work</h3>
          <p>
            Reserved for felling mature trees and processing large-diameter firewood. A longer bar needs a correspondingly powerful engine or motor to drive it — pairing a 20-inch bar with an underpowered saw is a common and dangerous mistake, since the chain will bog down and bind mid-cut, which is itself a kickback risk.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">A Simple Rule</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never use a bar longer than the tree diameter you actually cut most often. Bigger isn't safer — it's heavier, harder to control, and more prone to kickback when the tip contacts material unintentionally during awkward cuts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

