        <!-- SECTION 5: Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Chainsaw Safety: Kickback, PPE, and the Rules That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A chainsaw is statistically one of the most dangerous tools a homeowner will ever operate. This section isn't optional reading — treat it as seriously as the product reviews above, because the best chainsaw in the world is only as safe as the person running it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Understanding Kickback</h3>
          <p>
            Kickback happens when the upper portion of the bar tip contacts material unexpectedly, causing the saw to violently rotate back toward the operator's face and upper body in a fraction of a second — far faster than human reaction time allows you to move out of the way. It's the leading cause of serious chainsaw injuries. Modern saws mitigate the risk with low-kickback chain designs, reduced-radius bar tips, and a chain brake that stops the chain almost instantly when triggered by the sudden rotational force of a kickback event or manually by the operator's hand guard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Non-Negotiable PPE List</h3>
          <p>
            Before starting any chainsaw, you should be wearing cut-resistant chainsaw chaps or pants rated to stop a moving chain, a helmet system with integrated face screen and hearing protection, snug-fitting non-slip gloves, and steel-toe boots. This isn't excessive — chainsaw chaps in particular have prevented countless leg lacerations by jamming the chain the instant it contacts the protective fibers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Cut Above Shoulder Height</h3>
          <p>
            Cutting with a handheld chainsaw above shoulder height dramatically increases kickback risk and removes your ability to control the saw with proper body positioning. If a branch is above shoulder height, use a pole saw like the Greenworks PO80 reviewed above, or call a professional arborist. This single rule prevents more serious injuries than almost any other precaution on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Additional Safety Fundamentals</h3>
          <p>
            Always maintain a firm two-handed grip with your thumb wrapped under the front handle — never a thumbless grip. Keep a clear escape path planned before starting any felling cut. Never cut alone on a large property without someone aware of your location and expected return time. Keep bystanders and pets at least two tree-lengths away during felling. And never operate a chainsaw while fatigued, distracted, or under the influence of alcohol or medication that affects reaction time.
          </p>
        </div>

