            6. Chainsaw Maintenance Basics: Keep It Sharp and Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            A well-maintained chainsaw is a safer chainsaw — a dull chain forces you to apply extra pressure, which increases the risk of the saw slipping or binding unpredictably. Here's the maintenance routine that keeps any saw on this list performing like new.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chain Sharpening and Tension</h3>
          <p>
            Sharpen your chain every 1-2 hours of actual cutting time, or immediately if you notice the saw producing fine sawdust instead of clean chips, pulling to one side, or requiring extra downward force. A round file matched to your chain's specific pitch, used at the correct angle, takes just a few minutes per chain once you've done it a handful of times. Check chain tension before every use — a properly tensioned chain should sit snug against the bar but still pull freely by hand; too loose and it can derail mid-cut, too tight and it strains the bar and drive sprocket.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bar Oil</h3>
          <p>
            Every chainsaw, regardless of power source, needs bar and chain oil to lubricate the bar groove and reduce friction and heat. Check the oil reservoir before every session and top it off — running a saw dry, even briefly, accelerates bar wear dramatically and can damage the drive sprocket. Most of the saws on this list, including all the battery models, feature automatic oiling systems, but you still need to keep the reservoir filled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Air Filter (Gas Models)</h3>
          <p>
            Gas chainsaws pull air through a filter to feed the carburetor, and that filter clogs with sawdust surprisingly fast, especially in dry or dusty conditions. Check it every few hours of use and clean or replace it as needed — a clogged filter starves the engine of air, causing rough running, reduced power, and poor fuel efficiency.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Off-Season Storage</h3>
          <p>
            For gas saws, run the tank dry or add fuel stabilizer before storing for more than a month — ethanol-blended gas left sitting will gum up a carburetor and cause hard starting in spring. For battery saws, store batteries at roughly 40-80% charge in a cool, dry location rather than fully charged or fully depleted, which extends long-term battery health. For every saw type, back off chain tension slightly before extended storage and wipe the bar and chain with a light coat of oil to prevent rust.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Chainsaw Mistakes That Waste Money or Cause Injury
          </h2>

          <p>
            After reviewing hundreds of customer complaints and consulting with arborists during testing, these are the mistakes we saw most often — some costly, some genuinely dangerous.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Much Bar for the Job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Homeowners consistently overbuy bar length, assuming bigger equals better. A 20-inch bar on a saw meant primarily for pruning and light limbing is heavier, harder to control, and more prone to kickback when the extended tip catches on unintended material during awkward cuts. Match the bar to your actual most-common task, not your rare worst-case scenario — you can always rent or borrow a bigger saw for the one time a year you fell a large tree.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting With a Dull Chain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull chain is more dangerous than a sharp one, not less — it forces you to push harder into the cut, which increases the odds of the saw slipping, binding, or kicking back unpredictably when it finally bites. If you have to force the saw through wood rather than letting the chain do the work, stop and sharpen or replace the chain immediately rather than pushing through the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping PPE for a Quick Cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single most common cause of serious chainsaw injury in our research was operators skipping chaps or eye protection for what they assumed would be a thirty-second cut. Kickback and slip incidents happen fastest exactly when you're least prepared for them — the quick job with no PPE. Suit up every time, regardless of how small the cut seems.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Gas Sit in the Tank All Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ethanol-blended fuel left sitting in a carburetor over an off-season absorbs moisture and breaks down, leaving gummy varnish deposits that cause hard starting or complete carburetor failure by spring. This is the single most common reason gas chainsaws end up at a repair shop, and it's entirely preventable by running the tank dry or adding fuel stabilizer before storage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best chainsaw for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, a 16-18 inch battery chainsaw like the EGO Power+ CS1800 hits the best balance of power, low maintenance, and safety. It handles firewood processing and storm cleanup without the fuel mixing and pull-cord hassle of gas. If you fell large trees or process serious firewood volume every year, a gas chainsaw like the Stihline MS 462 Pro still offers more sustained power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas or battery chainsaw better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas chainsaws deliver more sustained power with no runtime limit, making them better for felling large trees and heavy all-day firewood cutting. Battery chainsaws are quieter, require far less maintenance, and are more than powerful enough for pruning, limbing, and moderate firewood processing. Most homeowners are better served by a quality battery chainsaw; professionals and serious firewood processors still lean toward gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size chainsaw bar do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For light pruning and limbing, a 12-14 inch bar is sufficient. For typical homeowner work like firewood processing and cutting trees up to about 16 inches in diameter, a 16-18 inch bar is the sweet spot. For felling larger trees over 20 inches in diameter, you need a 20-inch or longer bar paired with adequate engine power to drive it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I prevent chainsaw kickback?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep the saw's upper bar tip away from contact with wood or other objects, maintain a firm two-handed grip with your thumb wrapped under the front handle, use a saw equipped with a low-kickback chain and chain brake, keep the chain properly sharpened and tensioned, and never cut with the tip of the bar unless performing a controlled boring cut you've been trained to make.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I sharpen my chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sharpen your chain every 1-2 hours of actual cutting time, or immediately if you notice the saw pulling to one side, producing fine dust instead of wood chips, or requiring you to force the cut. Cutting in dirt or hitting a rock or nail dulls a chain instantly and calls for immediate sharpening or chain replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What safety gear do I need to run a chainsaw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At minimum: cut-resistant chainsaw chaps or pants, a helmet with integrated face screen and hearing protection, non-slip gloves, and steel-toe boots. Never operate a chainsaw solo without someone aware of your location, never cut above shoulder height, and always maintain a stable footing and clear escape path before starting a cut.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

